<document lang="en" type="presentation" theme-accent="blue">
  <title>Delta decks</title>

  <!-- M7 items 33 + 36: `<slide>` is one deck slide (its `<title>` shows at the
       top); the runtime deck controller pages one slide at a time with the arrow
       keys (←/→ or ↑/↓), Space/PageDown, and Home/End. With JS off, every slide
       stays visible and scrollable. Section→divider slides (item 34) are still to come. -->

  <!-- M7 item 35: opt into the progress bar — it renders as the line under each
       slide title, filling as you advance. Remove this and the line disappears. -->
  <progress/>

  <slide>
    <title>Presentations in Delta</title>
    The same strict-XML source, compiled to a single offline HTML file — now paged
    at projector scale. Use the arrow keys to move between slides.
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Math still works</title>
    Inline math like $e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0$ renders as usual, and display math is
    centered and large:
    <equation id="eq:gauss">\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^2}\,dx = \sqrt{\pi}</equation>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Theorems on a slide</title>
    <theorem id="thm:pyth">
      <title>Pythagoras</title>
      For a right triangle with legs $a$, $b$ and hypotenuse $c$,
      <equation id="eq:pyth">a^2 + b^2 = c^2</equation>
    </theorem>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Ordinary chrome, deck scale</title>
    Lists and emphasis all carry over — one idea per point:
    <list marker="number">
      <item>Built from the same tags you already know.</item>
      <item>Each &lt;slide&gt; fills the screen, its title at the top.</item>
      <item>Still a single, fully offline file.</item>
    </list>
  </slide>
</document>
